    "result_text": "All 22 tests pass, build green. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented `namesakeCount` on owner creation \u2014 the count of existing owners sharing the same `firstName` and `lastName` (case-insensitive), captured at create time before the new owner is saved, persisted, and returned on reads.\n\nChanges:\n- **`AssignNamesakeCount.java`** (new) \u2014 pipeline function that counts existing owners matching first+last name case-insensitively via `equalsIgnoreCase`, then mutates the built `Owner` in place. Runs before the owner is saved, so `findAll()` naturally excludes it.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `assignNamesakeCount` step (under `transaction` governance) between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, mirroring the existing `AssignCustomerCode` placement.\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `namesakeCount` field (`Integer`, `@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `namesakeCount` as a read-only `int32` (min 0) on the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct auto-maps it by name.\n- **schema.sql** (hsqldb / h2 / mysql / postgres) \u2014 added the nullable `namesake_count` column so pre-existing/seed owners load as `null`.\n\nThe `Owner` schema's computed field maps automatically through MapStruct (same field name), so no mapper change was needed. Full suite: 22/22 green.",
